The test suite is now in `tests/public/test_max_product.py`. `spec.md` still has only the title and starter code, so I wrote the tests against the published problem with that title, LeetCode 3509. The rules and constraints I used are written at the top of the file, so if the real statement differs, you can see exactly what was assumed. I took both the samples and the constraints from memory of LeetCode 3509, not from `spec.md`.

- **Samples:** the problem's three examples, one test each.
- **Boundary cases:**
  - one-element inputs that give a product of 0, a product of 12 (the largest element), no valid subsequence because the product goes over `limit`, and no valid subsequence because the sum can't reach `k`;
  - `k` at ±10⁵;
  - a product exactly equal to `limit` (allowed) and one just over it (rejected);
  - a 0 in the middle of the array, and a negative `k` that can't be reached;
  - 150-element arrays of all 12s and all 0s.
- **Expected values:** each test has a comment showing how its answer was worked out by hand. I also checked the small cases by listing every subsequence with a brute-force script. For the 150-element cases I worked the answers out by hand.

`python3 sandbox/compile_check.py` reports no errors. All 16 tests fail when run, as expected, because `src/solution.py` still raises `not implemented`.

16 tests, in 1 file.
